Great, I can see your `ReportGenerator.cs` on **.NET 10**. For `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, and `Directory.Exists`, the best approach is **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — a battle-tested NuGet package that provides a mockable `IFileSystem` interface. No need to write custom wrappers.

## Recommended Approach

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

Replace direct `File.*` / `Directory.*` calls with the injected abstraction:

```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ReportGenerator(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var outputDir = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REPORT_OUTPUT_DIR") ?? "/tmp/reports";

        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(outputDir))
        {
            fileSystem.Directory.CreateDirectory(outputDir);
        }

        var reportPath = fileSystem.Path.Combine(outputDir, $"{reportName}.txt");
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}";

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(reportPath, content);

        return reportPath;
    }
}
```

### 3. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

Install the test helpers in your test project:

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

Then write tests like:

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/tmp/reports", new MockDirectoryData() }
});

var generator = new ReportGenerator(mockFs);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.True(mockFs.File.Exists(path));
var content = mockFs.File.ReadAllText(path);
Assert.Contains("Sales", content);
```

`MockFileSystem` gives you a fully in-memory filesystem — no real disk I/O, no cleanup needed, and tests run in parallel safely.

> **Note:** Your `ReportGenerator` also uses `DateTime.UtcNow`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.WriteLine` — those are additional testability concerns. For `DateTime.UtcNow` on .NET 10, inject the built-in `TimeProvider`. For `Environment`/`Console`, custom thin wrappers work best. Want me to generate those too?
